module

public import Linglib.Data.Experiments.MaldonadoCulbertson2022
public import Studies.Harbour2016

/-!
# Maldonado and Culbertson (2022): The learnability of person systems

[maldonado-culbertson-2022] test in artificial language learning whether the constraints proposed
for person partitions are hard or soft. Without a clusivity contrast languages assimilate the
inclusive into the first person, and neither the second-inclusive nor the third-inclusive
tripartition is attested. For [harbour-2016] [both are underivable](##mem_image_setting_graph_iff),
while for Harley and Ritter's features, which include an addressee feature, the second-inclusive one
is a natural class and derivable though marked, and only the third-inclusive one is underivable (pp.
313–314). In Experiment 2 the third-inclusive system was learned significantly worse than both
others, but the second-inclusive one only marginally worse than the first-inclusive one (p. 318, the
coefficients of `contrasts`): the inclusive can form a natural class with the second person, and the
cost of the third-inclusive system is a bias against homophony outside natural classes (pp.
318–319). That cost falls on the one condition no feature set generates.

## Implementation notes

The plural categories are their participant sets, first exclusive `{speaker}`, inclusive
`{speaker, addressee}`, second `{addressee}` and third `∅`, and the features those of
`Harbour2016.ThreeFeature`, [±hearer] standing for Harley and Ritter's addressee feature. The
model coefficients are kept as printed data; the study does not restate the paper's reading of
them. Experiments 1 and 3 are not formalized.

## References

* [maldonado-culbertson-2022]
* [harbour-2016]
* [harley-ritter-2002]
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MaldonadoCulbertson2022

open Discourse Finset Harbour2016

/-! ### The conditions as partitions (Table 7, p. 315) -/

/-- The participant set of the category whose form the inclusive shares. -/
def Condition.host : Condition → Finset Role
  | .firstInclusive => {.speaker}
  | .secondInclusive => {.addressee}
  | .thirdInclusive => ∅

/-- The partition a condition teaches: every category its own form, except that the inclusive
shares the form of the condition's host category. -/
def Condition.graph (c : Condition) : Finset (Finset Role × Finset Role) :=
  univ.filter fun p ↦ p.1 = p.2 ∨
    ({p.1, p.2} : Finset (Finset Role)) = {{.speaker, .addressee}, c.host}

theorem graph_firstInclusive :
    Condition.firstInclusive.graph = Harbour2016.graph {.participant, .author} := by decide

theorem graph_secondInclusive :
    Condition.secondInclusive.graph = Harbour2016.graph {.participant, .hearer} := by decide

/-- A condition's partition is generated by some feature set iff it is not the third-inclusive
one, whose inclusive and third person share no feature value. -/
theorem generated_iff (c : Condition) :
    (∃ F, Harbour2016.graph F = c.graph) ↔ c ≠ .thirdInclusive := by
  revert c; decide

/-- Of the three, only the first-inclusive partition is generated by Harbour's parameters. -/
theorem mem_image_setting_graph_iff (c : Condition) :
    c.graph ∈ univ.image Harbour2016.Setting.graph ↔ c = .firstInclusive := by
  rw [Harbour2016.image_graph_eq]; revert c; decide

end MaldonadoCulbertson2022
